// tests/snippets/module_const_mutability — every module-const shape, read-only
// and marked, across the type kinds that can carry the marker.
//
// A module const has NO runtime storage: its value is rebuilt at each read site,
// fn-wrapped behind a synthetic accessor, or baked into `.rodata`. Three
// consequences this snippet pins down:
//
//   1. Read-only is the default whether or not a type is written. Unannotated
//      goes through `orchestrate::auto_const_module_array`; annotated goes
//      through the read-only default in `declare_const`. Both end up frozen —
//      before they agreed, an annotated array silently lost its `.rodata`
//      pooling and went back to being rebuilt op-by-op at every read.
//   2. `!` on a module const does NOT grant mutation — a write through it is
//      T3070 regardless (see `t3070_element_through_module_const`). What it
//      does is keep the value OUT of the data pool, which is the fn-wrap /
//      rebuild path (`const_fn_wrap` guards that side).
//   3. Only a type with an interior takes the marker. `MAX: i32!` and
//      `MODE: Mode!` are T3075 — see `t3075_marker_on_value_type`.
//
// The storage each shape lands in is visible in `bytecode.snapshot.virt`. The
// pool holds ARRAYS: a read-only one becomes a `data` entry read by `data.const`
// (primitive elements as a packed blob, struct elements as an aggregate), a
// marked one an `array.new` sequence. A lone struct or tuple is not a pool
// candidate today whatever its mutability, and a primitive / enum const is
// inlined at each use site.

module "snippet"

import "std/io"

Spec :: struct {
    name: string
    code: i32
}

Mode :: enum(u8) { Fast, Small }

// ---- read-only : the default, annotated or not ----------------------------

// Primitive — no interior, so no marker is possible. Inlined at each use site.
MAX: i32: 42
// Inferred, no annotation at all.
GREETING :: "hello"
// Enum — a value through and through, like a primitive.
MODE: Mode: .Fast
// Primitive-element array, annotated : frozen by the read-only default, so it
// bakes into `.rodata`.
DIGITS: string[]: ["0", "1", "2"]
// Same, unannotated : frozen by the auto-pin. Same storage.
LIMITS :: [10, 20, 30]
// Struct element — pooled as an aggregate.
SPECS: Spec[]: [
    Spec { .name = "alpha", .code = 1 },
    Spec { .name = "beta",  .code = 2 },
]
// A struct, not an array. Read-only by default too.
ORIGIN: Spec: Spec { .name = "origin", .code = 0 }
// A tuple carries the bit per level like an array does.
PAIR: [i32, string]: [7, "seven"]

// ---- marked : out of the data pool ---------------------------------------

// The `!` opts out of pooling — the value is rebuilt / fn-wrapped instead. It
// grants no mutation: `SCRATCH.push(4)` here would still be T3071.
SCRATCH: i32[]!: [1, 2, 3]
// Marked at the ELEMENT level instead of the array level: a different type
// (`Spec![]` vs `Spec[]!`), and the array itself stays read-only.
TEMPLATES: Spec![]: [Spec { .name = "tpl", .code = 9 }]

main :: fn() -> i32 {
    println("${MAX} ${GREETING} ${MODE == Mode.Fast}")
    println("${DIGITS[1]} ${LIMITS[2]} ${SPECS[1].name} ${ORIGIN.name}")
    println("${PAIR.0}${PAIR.1}")
    println("${SCRATCH[0]} ${TEMPLATES[0].code}")
    // Reading through any of them is always fine — only writes are rejected.
    total := 0
    for v in LIMITS {
        total = total + v
    }
    println("total=${total}")
    return 0
}
